00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:20.970
And that's where
Dremel came along.

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:23.130
So Dremel, for the
past 10-plus years,

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:28.210
has been powering Google's data
and analytics infrastructure.

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:30.720
And as most people in
this room already know,

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:32.920
BigQuery is powered by Dremel.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:34.410
BigQuery is Dremel.

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:36.100
It's the same engineering team.

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:40.130
It's the same infrastructure,
the same codebase.

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:44.110
And so when you think about it,
when you're running BigQuery,

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:46.400
you're running on top
of Google's innovation.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:48.830
You're running on top of Dremel.

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:53.810
What gives BigQuery its
scale characteristics

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:56.900
is its native
cloud architecture,

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:59.150
cloud-native architecture,
its unique architectural

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:01.550
principles, unique
architectural considerations.

00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:04.220
So it starts with a stateless
multi-tenant compute cluster,

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:06.980
which is really what
Dremel is, which

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:10.410
is a really unique offering.

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:13.470
And it's joined together by
a logical storage system that

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:16.320
scales into petabytes and more.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.380
And it's all wired
together by our network

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:20.550
that scales to petabytes.

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:23.010
And then we started
to go beyond that.

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:26.130
We separate compute and
state, intermediate state

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:28.680
during processing,
which accelerates many

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:31.350
of our queries, gives us
performance durability,

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:34.380
scalability characteristics.

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:36.270
And then we allow to
share data in place

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:39.720
without creating data silos,
without moving data around

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:40.670
needlessly.

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:42.600
You bring analytics to data.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:44.839
You bring users to data,
not the other way around.

00:02:44.839 --> 00:02:46.380
And finally, we
wrapped it all around

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:49.230
in a fully managed offering
that minimizes complexity,

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:51.360
manageability, overhead
for your organizations.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:54.810
Makes it easy for you guys to
use this type of technology

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:55.900
at scale.

