00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:42.410
So I thought I'd start with
a very brief history of--

00:05:42.410 --> 00:05:45.140
the short history of Spotify
and the cloud, which actually,

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:47.952
now that I think about it, would
be a great name for a band.

00:05:47.952 --> 00:05:49.891
I'll have to do that some day.

00:05:49.891 --> 00:05:50.390
All right.

00:05:50.390 --> 00:05:54.200
So four years ago when
we started the transition

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:57.530
to the cloud--

00:05:57.530 --> 00:06:00.410
at that point, Spotify was
almost completely on-premise

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:01.820
and bare-metal.

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:04.670
We were doing--

00:06:04.670 --> 00:06:08.450
I think at our largest, point we
had something like 2,500 nodes

00:06:08.450 --> 00:06:11.930
in our Hadoop cluster and over
10,000 machines and four data

00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:13.299
centers around the world.

00:06:13.299 --> 00:06:14.840
And what we were
finding is that even

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:16.548
at that kind of scale,
one of the largest

00:06:16.548 --> 00:06:19.890
Hadoop clusters in Europe, it
still really wasn't enough.

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:21.830
We were expanding
rapidly in the US,

00:06:21.830 --> 00:06:23.270
and we were really
having trouble

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:25.430
keeping up with the
growth and being

00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:27.700
able to do our analytical work.

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:30.680
And that, in particular, was a
really big pain point for us.

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:33.890
Running a single query
on this cluster in hive

00:06:33.890 --> 00:06:36.050
could take us hours.

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:38.300
So after a couple of years
of small experiments,

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:42.730
we finally decided to take the
plunge and moved to the cloud.

00:06:42.730 --> 00:06:45.470
But before I move on, I
wanted to give a brief plug

00:06:45.470 --> 00:06:48.220
to my amazing colleagues,
Josh and Ramon, who

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:50.930
will be giving a talk about
[INAUDIBLE] migration tomorrow

00:06:50.930 --> 00:06:51.900
afternoon.

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:54.332
So if you have any interest
in hearing more about how

00:06:54.332 --> 00:06:55.790
we did that migration
to the cloud,

00:06:55.790 --> 00:06:57.081
please listen in to their talk.

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:01.820
So for us, the move to
BigQuery was actually

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:03.410
a really big success.

00:07:03.410 --> 00:07:06.830
It was rapidly adopted by the
Spotify analytics and data

00:07:06.830 --> 00:07:08.104
science community.

00:07:08.104 --> 00:07:10.520
And to this date, it still
remains one of the most popular

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:13.310
GCP tools we use at Spotify.

00:07:13.310 --> 00:07:15.470
For us in particular,
what it really meant

00:07:15.470 --> 00:07:17.870
for us was that we were
getting fast results.

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:20.300
We were able to add
capacity a lot easier.

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:22.610
And we found that the
integration with the other GCP

00:07:22.610 --> 00:07:25.580
tools was really help.

00:07:25.580 --> 00:07:28.070
As well, one of the biggest
wins for us was really

00:07:28.070 --> 00:07:31.910
how it democratized
access to data at Spotify.

00:07:31.910 --> 00:07:34.760
It was really that ease of use
meant that more people could

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:38.370
be asking more questions and
getting responses faster,

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:40.970
which meant that overall
we are getting a massively

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:42.920
shorter time to insight.

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:45.770
Using SQL particularly as a
querying language was really

00:07:45.770 --> 00:07:48.480
powerful for letting
people across the company

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:50.270
ask questions of
our data in a way

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:53.780
that they'd never been
able to do before.

00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:56.320
So I thought I'd give you
a quick example of how

00:07:56.320 --> 00:07:58.180
using BigQuery
impacted our ability

00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:00.260
to ask questions of our data.

00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:03.280
So this is a kind of question
somebody, a data scientist,

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:04.570
might want to be asking.

00:08:04.570 --> 00:08:08.620
How much time did users in
Spain spend listening to Spotify

00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:11.920
in October of 2016?

00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:13.540
Before when we were
working with Hive,

00:08:13.540 --> 00:08:16.030
it would take us something
like 16 minutes or so

00:08:16.030 --> 00:08:19.580
to get an answer for
this one question.

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:22.750
Now when we moved to
BigQuery, the same question

00:08:22.750 --> 00:08:25.460
only takes us about 33 seconds.

00:08:25.460 --> 00:08:28.150
So as you can see, a huge
sea change in our ability

00:08:28.150 --> 00:08:29.800
to get those answers quickly.

00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:32.919
Now, if you multiply that by
hundreds of questions asked

00:08:32.919 --> 00:08:35.740
by hundreds of people
at Spotify daily,

00:08:35.740 --> 00:08:37.299
the kind of impact
this kind of speed

00:08:37.299 --> 00:08:38.782
is going to have
is really clear.

00:08:38.782 --> 00:08:40.240
And in case you
guys are wondering,

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:42.400
the answer to that
question is the total hours

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:47.170
are equivalent to
nearly 10,000 years.

00:08:47.170 --> 00:08:49.510
All right, so what
does it really mean

00:08:49.510 --> 00:08:52.490
or what do we mean when
we say petabyte scale?

00:08:52.490 --> 00:08:54.130
What does that really look like?

00:08:54.130 --> 00:08:56.680
Internally in Spotify we
sometimes refer to this as,

00:08:56.680 --> 00:08:58.630
how big is data?

00:08:58.630 --> 00:09:02.330
And our answer for that
is really, really big.

00:09:02.330 --> 00:09:08.260
So right now Spotify has
about 170 million active users

00:09:08.260 --> 00:09:12.700
listening to our catalog of
over 35 million songs in 65

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:13.910
different markets.

00:09:13.910 --> 00:09:17.530
So as you can probably imagine,
we generate a lot of data.

00:09:17.530 --> 00:09:20.320
And being able to ask the right
questions in the right way

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:22.200
becomes really important to us.

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:26.020
And so that's what we end
up using BigQuery for.

00:09:26.020 --> 00:09:30.220
So we use BigQuery to do that
kind of analysis at a scale

00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:32.200
that we've never been
able to do before.

00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:35.470
Right now we have about 25%
of employees at BigQuery

00:09:35.470 --> 00:09:38.470
that are using BigQuery on a
regular basis, either directly

00:09:38.470 --> 00:09:41.200
or through custom tooling
on top of BigQuery.

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:44.560
We process around 400 petabytes
of data monthly and store

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:47.647
over 100 petabytes of data,
specifically in BigQuery.

00:09:47.647 --> 00:09:49.146
Doesn't even talk
about things we're

00:09:49.146 --> 00:09:52.780
storing in other parts
of the GCP product suite.

00:09:52.780 --> 00:09:55.540
And that also includes loading
in around 500 terabytes

00:09:55.540 --> 00:09:58.690
of new data daily into BigQuery.

00:09:58.690 --> 00:10:00.550
And what that means
is that in total we

00:10:00.550 --> 00:10:02.660
end up running around 6
million queries a month.

00:10:02.660 --> 00:10:04.480
So that's a really big scale.

00:10:07.820 --> 00:10:10.780
So for us, that move to
BigQuery has actually

00:10:10.780 --> 00:10:12.350
solved a lot of
the issues that we

00:10:12.350 --> 00:10:15.310
used to have in our old
clusters, but as we've grown,

00:10:15.310 --> 00:10:17.680
we really had to
develop new techniques

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:20.830
to ensure that we're going
to continue to be successful.

00:10:20.830 --> 00:10:22.600
For us, we actually
see this as a big win.

00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:25.530
We've moved beyond those
lower level problems of,

00:10:25.530 --> 00:10:28.180
can we even get the answers
we need, to the higher level

00:10:28.180 --> 00:10:30.340
questions of, how do
we continue to ensure

00:10:30.340 --> 00:10:34.194
good performance if our
data doubles in size again?

00:10:34.194 --> 00:10:35.860
So I thought I'd start
with a few things

00:10:35.860 --> 00:10:38.680
here that we've discovered
over the past year that

00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:42.570
have helped us scale
effectively in BigQuery.

00:10:42.570 --> 00:10:44.550
We found that the
things we're doing

00:10:44.550 --> 00:10:49.050
fall into four categories, the
first being administration,

00:10:49.050 --> 00:10:52.150
managing how BigQuery is
being used across the company.

00:10:52.150 --> 00:10:54.090
Second one is around
education, learning

00:10:54.090 --> 00:10:56.610
about how to tune our BigQuery
performance to get the best

00:10:56.610 --> 00:10:58.570
results.

00:10:58.570 --> 00:11:00.110
Third is integration.

00:11:00.110 --> 00:11:03.990
That's leveraging the APIs
and other GCP products

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:06.990
to get the right tool
for the right time.

00:11:06.990 --> 00:11:10.590
And then finally, we have
working closely with Google

00:11:10.590 --> 00:11:14.900
to improve BigQuery, so working
on our partnership with them.

00:11:14.900 --> 00:11:17.980
So I'm going to dig a little
bit deeper into each of those.

00:11:17.980 --> 00:11:20.370
First of all, when we talk
about the kinds of things

00:11:20.370 --> 00:11:22.870
we are doing in administration
to be successful,

00:11:22.870 --> 00:11:24.300
one of the first
things we did was

00:11:24.300 --> 00:11:26.430
move to a fixed-capacity model.

00:11:26.430 --> 00:11:27.990
This has been
really helpful to us

00:11:27.990 --> 00:11:31.289
because it ensures that
we have dedicated slots.

00:11:31.289 --> 00:11:33.205
And it helps us be
predictable with our costs.

00:11:33.205 --> 00:11:35.117
And that's been
a big win for us.

00:11:35.117 --> 00:11:37.200
That does mean we need to
think a little bit ahead

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:39.330
to enable to grow our
capacity, but we've

00:11:39.330 --> 00:11:41.970
been finding that it's actually
significantly still better

00:11:41.970 --> 00:11:46.260
than the problems we were
having in our on-premise Hadoop

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:48.010
clusters for a growing capacity.

00:11:48.010 --> 00:11:51.610
We still have a significantly
faster speed of growth there.

00:11:51.610 --> 00:11:55.650
The second thing we do is, we've
been creating sub-reservations,

00:11:55.650 --> 00:12:00.960
which are kind of smaller pools
within our larger reservation.

00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:05.760
That allows us to isolate jobs
and business-critical projects.

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:07.710
That's actually been
really helpful for us,

00:12:07.710 --> 00:12:09.330
particularly
because we regularly

00:12:09.330 --> 00:12:11.780
rebalance which projects
have access to which pools.

00:12:11.780 --> 00:12:13.530
And that means that
we're getting the best

00:12:13.530 --> 00:12:16.210
performance for the right time.

00:12:16.210 --> 00:12:17.880
A good example of
this is whenever

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:20.310
we are going to do a large
backfill on our data--

00:12:20.310 --> 00:12:23.430
something went wrong or
we need to add some data

00:12:23.430 --> 00:12:25.720
into a table that
already exists--

00:12:25.720 --> 00:12:27.480
we will move that
particular project

00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:30.720
into its own sub-reservation
with an isolated set

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:32.940
of resources that it can use.

00:12:32.940 --> 00:12:35.040
And that means it's
preventing that

00:12:35.040 --> 00:12:37.320
from impacting the day-to-day
work of everybody else,

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:39.870
but also ensuring that
that particular job has

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:42.030
the resources and needs
to complete on time.

00:12:42.030 --> 00:12:45.000
That's been really
powerful for us.

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:48.550
We also make sure that
business-critical projects are

00:12:48.550 --> 00:12:49.759
in their own sub-reservation.

00:12:49.759 --> 00:12:51.758
That means no matter what
happens they're always

00:12:51.758 --> 00:12:54.355
going to have access to the
resources they need to complete

00:12:54.355 --> 00:12:55.605
those business-critical tasks.

00:12:58.170 --> 00:12:59.670
The next thing we've
been working on

00:12:59.670 --> 00:13:03.285
has to do with
education, specifically,

00:13:03.285 --> 00:13:05.160
learning how to tune
our performance as being

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:06.900
one of the main things.

00:13:06.900 --> 00:13:09.210
For us what that really
means is learning

00:13:09.210 --> 00:13:11.820
how do we need to
manage our concurrency?

00:13:11.820 --> 00:13:14.460
How do we use batch
versus interactive mode

00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:17.250
to help figure out
when jobs should run?

00:13:17.250 --> 00:13:18.930
These kinds of
things help us ensure

00:13:18.930 --> 00:13:21.450
that we're getting the
best performance when we're

00:13:21.450 --> 00:13:24.760
running things on BigQuery.

00:13:24.760 --> 00:13:26.890
The second thing we been
learning a lot about

00:13:26.890 --> 00:13:29.620
is the impact of
Dremel architecture,

00:13:29.620 --> 00:13:33.610
understanding that it's
not a relational database

00:13:33.610 --> 00:13:35.770
and that using a
columnar data store

00:13:35.770 --> 00:13:38.500
actually provides significant
benefits for certain kinds

00:13:38.500 --> 00:13:40.660
of queries but can
be very different

00:13:40.660 --> 00:13:43.690
and not work as well for
other kinds of queries.

00:13:43.690 --> 00:13:46.960
And so ensuring that people who
are running queries on BigQuery

00:13:46.960 --> 00:13:49.570
across the company
really understand

00:13:49.570 --> 00:13:51.490
how to optimize for the
kind of architecture

00:13:51.490 --> 00:13:53.490
they're running on.

00:13:53.490 --> 00:13:56.190
And finally, the thing
we do within Spotify

00:13:56.190 --> 00:14:00.390
is we have a very passionate
community of BigQuery users,

00:14:00.390 --> 00:14:02.700
and we leverage
that a lot to help

00:14:02.700 --> 00:14:06.960
share best practices
around the company.

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:09.660
Next, we work on integration.

00:14:09.660 --> 00:14:12.720
Specifically, we use
BigQuery API wrappers

00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:15.090
to allow us to build our
own set of custom tools

00:14:15.090 --> 00:14:17.790
on top of BigQuery to
meet our own needs.

00:14:17.790 --> 00:14:20.460
This really allows us
to do deeper integration

00:14:20.460 --> 00:14:22.410
with our own tools
and environment

00:14:22.410 --> 00:14:24.470
while still leveraging
that power of BigQuery

00:14:24.470 --> 00:14:25.294
behind the scenes.

00:14:25.294 --> 00:14:27.210
We find that that's been
really helpful for us

00:14:27.210 --> 00:14:31.350
to continue growing and
fine tuning something

00:14:31.350 --> 00:14:34.650
for our specific needs.

00:14:34.650 --> 00:14:39.330
We also leverage integrations
with other Google Cloud data

00:14:39.330 --> 00:14:41.250
products and
specifically the ability

00:14:41.250 --> 00:14:44.550
to move data between
different products easily.

00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:45.930
That allows us to
really optimize

00:14:45.930 --> 00:14:50.130
by using the right
tool for the job.

00:14:50.130 --> 00:14:53.330
Finally, we focus on building a
good partnership, specifically

00:14:53.330 --> 00:14:56.390
with the Google BigQuery
team, as well with

00:14:56.390 --> 00:15:00.110
our strategic cloud engineers
that work with us here

00:15:00.110 --> 00:15:02.090
at Spotify.

00:15:02.090 --> 00:15:04.610
We work with them really to
help keep expanding the features

00:15:04.610 --> 00:15:07.010
and capabilities of
BigQuery that you really

00:15:07.010 --> 00:15:09.440
need to be able to operate
at this kind of scale.

00:15:09.440 --> 00:15:11.330
What that means for us
on a day-to-day basis

00:15:11.330 --> 00:15:14.130
is that we're talking with
them about feature requests.

00:15:14.130 --> 00:15:16.970
And as we're pushing the limits
of what BigQuery does right

00:15:16.970 --> 00:15:19.004
now, we work closely
in telling them,

00:15:19.004 --> 00:15:20.670
hey, these are the
new features we need.

00:15:20.670 --> 00:15:22.550
This is where we'd
like to see this grow.

00:15:22.550 --> 00:15:25.640
A great example of this
for us is, not too long ago

00:15:25.640 --> 00:15:30.220
we were working on becoming
ready for GDPR, the General

00:15:30.220 --> 00:15:31.970
Data Protection
Regulation that rolled out

00:15:31.970 --> 00:15:34.130
in Europe a few months ago.

00:15:34.130 --> 00:15:36.710
And we realized
that the technique

00:15:36.710 --> 00:15:39.260
we were using for
encryption and decryption

00:15:39.260 --> 00:15:41.990
just wasn't going to
scale to the extent we

00:15:41.990 --> 00:15:44.840
needed for DPR compliance.

00:15:44.840 --> 00:15:47.390
So we worked closely
with the BigQuery team

00:15:47.390 --> 00:15:49.370
to work on new tools
and features that

00:15:49.370 --> 00:15:52.970
allow us to do that at the scale
we needed to and at the speed

00:15:52.970 --> 00:15:54.480
we needed to do, as well.

00:15:54.480 --> 00:15:56.057
That's been really helpful.

00:15:56.057 --> 00:15:57.890
And then finally, we've
been working closely

00:15:57.890 --> 00:16:00.830
with the support team
at Google to help us

00:16:00.830 --> 00:16:02.450
with troubleshooting.

00:16:02.450 --> 00:16:05.900
Specifically, whenever we run
into any issues or users have

00:16:05.900 --> 00:16:08.540
challenges or we have
performance issues,

00:16:08.540 --> 00:16:11.390
we're able to really reach
out and get quick responses

00:16:11.390 --> 00:16:14.000
and solve our problems
very smoothly.

00:16:17.310 --> 00:16:18.350
And that's it for me.

00:16:18.350 --> 00:16:22.164
I'm gonna pass it over to Tino
to talk about the next person.

00:16:22.164 --> 00:16:25.116
[APPLAUSE]

00:16:25.116 --> 00:16:26.838
Who's next?

00:16:26.838 --> 00:16:29.544
TINO TERESHKO: I'm next.

00:16:29.544 --> 00:16:32.010
Hold on.

00:16:32.010 --> 00:16:33.880
We're good.

00:16:33.880 --> 00:16:34.630
Thank you, Nicole.

00:16:34.630 --> 00:16:35.850
That was fantastic.

00:16:35.850 --> 00:16:40.170
So Spotify has-- you
know, at one point in time

00:16:40.170 --> 00:16:41.880
before moving to
Google Cloud Platform,

00:16:41.880 --> 00:16:46.350
Spotify had the largest
Hadoop cluster in Europe

00:16:46.350 --> 00:16:47.520
or right up there.

00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:49.770
And highly tuned, they
spent a lot of time thinking

00:16:49.770 --> 00:16:53.190
about performance and
operational complexity.

00:16:53.190 --> 00:16:58.170
And now they're working with us
to solve their problems, which

00:16:58.170 --> 00:17:00.090
gives them an
opportunity to focus

00:17:00.090 --> 00:17:04.140
on delighting their customers
rather than trying to tweak

00:17:04.140 --> 00:17:07.619
certain HDFS variables or things
that they may not think are

00:17:07.619 --> 00:17:10.480
important for their business.

00:17:10.480 --> 00:17:14.250
Well, Spotify has been with
us for three years now.

00:17:14.250 --> 00:17:17.730
But I'd like to welcome
onstage our next customer

00:17:17.730 --> 00:17:20.069
here, our next speaker,
Tuan, from Oath.

00:17:23.579 --> 00:17:26.909
You probably use a property
of Oath every single day

00:17:26.909 --> 00:17:27.450
in your life.

00:17:30.280 --> 00:17:35.880
And Oath has been with
us on stage the last time

00:17:35.880 --> 00:17:37.630
and they've kind of
grown since then.

00:17:37.630 --> 00:17:39.005
So it's really
interesting to see

00:17:39.005 --> 00:17:40.680
the progression of
where they're going

00:17:40.680 --> 00:17:42.300
and where they're
going to be next.

00:17:42.300 --> 00:17:44.928
Tuan, please.

00:17:44.928 --> 00:17:46.404
[APPLAUSE]

00:17:46.404 --> 00:17:49.360
TUAN PHAM: Thanks.

00:17:49.360 --> 00:17:50.410
Thank you, Tino.

00:17:50.410 --> 00:17:52.320
It's like he said,
my name is Tuan Pham,

00:17:52.320 --> 00:17:56.120
and I'm a technical director at
Oath on the Media and Analytics

00:17:56.120 --> 00:17:59.780
Warehouse team,
also known as MAW.

00:17:59.780 --> 00:18:02.480
And for those of you who are
not familiar or as familiar

00:18:02.480 --> 00:18:04.880
with Oath, it was
basically formed last year

00:18:04.880 --> 00:18:08.690
after Verizon's acquisition of
Yahoo, which merged us together

00:18:08.690 --> 00:18:10.280
with AOL.

00:18:10.280 --> 00:18:13.430
And that formed a kind
of house of brands,

00:18:13.430 --> 00:18:16.280
represented here by what
we like to show as orbs.

00:18:16.280 --> 00:18:18.230
Not an exhaustive
list, but we have

00:18:18.230 --> 00:18:21.150
a lot of well-known
properties, products,

00:18:21.150 --> 00:18:25.254
including Huffington Post,
Flurry, Tumblr, Tech Crunch,

00:18:25.254 --> 00:18:26.420
and Engadget, to name a few.

00:18:29.730 --> 00:18:32.050
So when we talk about
media analytics,

00:18:32.050 --> 00:18:33.980
Oath, what we're
really talking about

00:18:33.980 --> 00:18:38.420
is scale in terms of volume
and velocity of data.

00:18:38.420 --> 00:18:40.010
We've spent the
last year working

00:18:40.010 --> 00:18:43.100
with all the brands
from the merger,

00:18:43.100 --> 00:18:47.090
trying to, from around the world
and across their platforms,

00:18:47.090 --> 00:18:49.540
conforming their data
and to bring it together

00:18:49.540 --> 00:18:54.180
as it presents a uniform
business view of our company.

00:18:54.180 --> 00:18:57.470
We also try to serve a wide
range of users and use cases.

00:18:57.470 --> 00:19:00.860
We provide KPI reporting
for executives,

00:19:00.860 --> 00:19:03.910
product metrics for product
managers and engineers.

00:19:03.910 --> 00:19:08.270
And we also support editors by
providing real-time information

00:19:08.270 --> 00:19:11.090
on the performance
of their content.

00:19:11.090 --> 00:19:13.250
And so to satisfy
all these use cases

00:19:13.250 --> 00:19:17.930
we try to strive to provide
a single self-service,

00:19:17.930 --> 00:19:20.240
interactive, and
scalable data platform.

00:19:23.810 --> 00:19:26.310
So I'll give a little history
about how we got to this point

00:19:26.310 --> 00:19:29.232
and before we had BigQuery.

00:19:29.232 --> 00:19:31.440
Generally at companies when
you start with analytics,

00:19:31.440 --> 00:19:32.370
you start small.

00:19:32.370 --> 00:19:34.495
You have a single pipeline
presenting the data

00:19:34.495 --> 00:19:34.995
to analysts.

00:19:37.771 --> 00:19:39.270
And then you bring
on more products.

00:19:39.270 --> 00:19:40.440
You have more requirements.

00:19:40.440 --> 00:19:44.940
And you start to add another
stack, another warehouse.

00:19:44.940 --> 00:19:45.786
Companies grow.

00:19:45.786 --> 00:19:47.160
You acquire other
businesses that

00:19:47.160 --> 00:19:49.339
bring in their own warehouses.

00:19:49.339 --> 00:19:51.630
These warehouses then start
to interact with each other

00:19:51.630 --> 00:19:56.010
and you have to kind of
reconcile that information.

00:19:56.010 --> 00:19:57.990
And so you kind of
land at a picture that

00:19:57.990 --> 00:19:59.070
looks kind of like this.

00:19:59.070 --> 00:20:01.860
You have a lot of
specialized data stores

00:20:01.860 --> 00:20:04.710
that have evolved
into data silos.

00:20:04.710 --> 00:20:06.180
You have a lot of
engineering teams

00:20:06.180 --> 00:20:10.290
kind of repeating work, which
is not very cost-efficient.

00:20:10.290 --> 00:20:12.840
You load data into
multiple places

00:20:12.840 --> 00:20:14.460
using different
logic to process it,

00:20:14.460 --> 00:20:16.489
sometimes yielding
different results.

00:20:16.489 --> 00:20:18.030
And then those
differences eventually

00:20:18.030 --> 00:20:20.797
have to be reconciled somehow.

00:20:20.797 --> 00:20:23.380
You have [INAUDIBLE] [? text ?]
[? access ?] starts to evolve.

00:20:23.380 --> 00:20:25.660
They use technologies
like relational databases

00:20:25.660 --> 00:20:27.720
that when you reach
a certain threshold,

00:20:27.720 --> 00:20:31.650
can't maintain with the pace
of the services required.

00:20:31.650 --> 00:20:34.800
And so overall you have
a very poor foundation

00:20:34.800 --> 00:20:36.260
for building an
analytics platform.

00:20:39.700 --> 00:20:41.410
So what was the solution?

00:20:41.410 --> 00:20:44.880
So Yahoo, obviously being a
huge contributor to Hadoop,

00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:48.330
had the expertise to build out
large scale clusters restoring

00:20:48.330 --> 00:20:50.550
data, large ETL pipelines.

00:20:50.550 --> 00:20:54.580
You know, we have clusters in
tens of thousands of nodes.

00:20:54.580 --> 00:20:55.710
So that was not the issue.

00:20:55.710 --> 00:20:58.710
And what we really needed
was a complimentary service

00:20:58.710 --> 00:21:03.060
that could take that data,
expose it, make it interactive,

00:21:03.060 --> 00:21:05.260
scale it with the
needs of the business,

00:21:05.260 --> 00:21:07.223
and then also be kind
of cost-efficient.

00:21:10.540 --> 00:21:13.050
So for us that
solution was BigQuery.

00:21:13.050 --> 00:21:16.260
We used that as the basis
for our new architecture.

00:21:16.260 --> 00:21:17.740
It's able to scale
with the needs

00:21:17.740 --> 00:21:21.780
as we brought on all the
brands from our merger.

00:21:21.780 --> 00:21:24.090
It's flexible enough in
terms of capabilities

00:21:24.090 --> 00:21:25.590
to satisfy the
different use cases

00:21:25.590 --> 00:21:28.660
that we have for our users.

00:21:28.660 --> 00:21:30.390
It's a managed
platform, so it brings

00:21:30.390 --> 00:21:31.850
lower operational overheads.

00:21:31.850 --> 00:21:35.220
And so it maintains relatively
lower costs for us as well.

00:21:40.160 --> 00:21:42.130
So initially when we
started our pipeline,

00:21:42.130 --> 00:21:45.040
it was a very basic
Hadoop design.

00:21:45.040 --> 00:21:48.820
We ingested our log data and
the bin data into our cluster,

00:21:48.820 --> 00:21:52.810
processed that with [? Big, ?]
staged it up in Hive,

00:21:52.810 --> 00:21:56.200
and then serviced it to our end
users through our BI analytics

00:21:56.200 --> 00:21:58.990
front end tool, which is Looker.

00:21:58.990 --> 00:22:01.720
And this served its purpose
when we started off.

00:22:01.720 --> 00:22:04.630
And if you remember the
original chain, when you start

00:22:04.630 --> 00:22:06.940
small everything works fine.

00:22:06.940 --> 00:22:09.220
And as Nicole said, they
started with Hive as well.

00:22:09.220 --> 00:22:12.277
But as demands grew,
Hive couldn't keep up

00:22:12.277 --> 00:22:14.110
with the services that
we needed to provide.

00:22:17.860 --> 00:22:20.784
So in looking at alternatives,
we started to look at BigQuery.

00:22:20.784 --> 00:22:22.450
And actually, this
diagram was presented

00:22:22.450 --> 00:22:25.810
at last year's conference by
my colleague, Nikhil Mishra.

00:22:25.810 --> 00:22:28.330
As our first step for
introducing BigQuery,

00:22:28.330 --> 00:22:30.985
we wanted to take a very
measured approach for adopting

00:22:30.985 --> 00:22:33.100
it into our platform.

00:22:33.100 --> 00:22:35.740
This gave us the opportunity
to work with the dev team,

00:22:35.740 --> 00:22:37.360
to understand the
GCP environment,

00:22:37.360 --> 00:22:42.754
to understand how that worked
with our Hadoop environment,

00:22:42.754 --> 00:22:44.170
let us understand
the capabilities

00:22:44.170 --> 00:22:45.970
and the shortcomings
of the tools

00:22:45.970 --> 00:22:48.910
and be able to figure out the
right way to integrate it.

00:22:48.910 --> 00:22:52.720
We left it in there along
with Hive, and still serving

00:22:52.720 --> 00:22:56.170
the data up through Looker,
so that our end users really

00:22:56.170 --> 00:22:58.262
didn't experience any
difference in their service,

00:22:58.262 --> 00:23:00.220
except maybe some possible
gains in performance

00:23:00.220 --> 00:23:01.551
and functionality.

00:23:05.610 --> 00:23:08.804
And then it led to our current
design, represented here.

00:23:08.804 --> 00:23:10.470
I think the biggest
thing you can notice

00:23:10.470 --> 00:23:13.590
is all the data now is being
served up through BigQuery.

00:23:13.590 --> 00:23:15.120
We've greatly
increased the capacity

00:23:15.120 --> 00:23:16.494
that we store in
there because we

00:23:16.494 --> 00:23:18.540
have brought in all this
data and all the brands

00:23:18.540 --> 00:23:20.300
from our merger.

00:23:20.300 --> 00:23:21.930
We've added in a
real-time pipeline

00:23:21.930 --> 00:23:23.880
into our system, which
allows us to take

00:23:23.880 --> 00:23:27.510
advantage of BigQuery streaming
ingestion capabilities.

00:23:27.510 --> 00:23:30.000
We've also started to take
advantage of additional GCP

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:32.400
services, like
PubSub, data flow,

00:23:32.400 --> 00:23:35.070
and data prop because of how
well they work together inside

00:23:35.070 --> 00:23:36.900
of that ecosystem.

00:23:36.900 --> 00:23:39.144
And really, just overall
presents a simpler design

00:23:39.144 --> 00:23:41.560
for the overall architecture
that we're trying to achieve.

00:23:44.500 --> 00:23:45.980
And like I said,
that architecture

00:23:45.980 --> 00:23:48.974
afforded us the ability
to simplify the design.

00:23:48.974 --> 00:23:50.890
We've gotten rid of all
those specialized data

00:23:50.890 --> 00:23:53.840
stores and data silos.

00:23:53.840 --> 00:23:56.162
We're able to unify things
like the streaming and batch

00:23:56.162 --> 00:23:58.120
pipelines so they all
are [? logic ?] [? and ?]

00:23:58.120 --> 00:24:00.910
centralized in a
single location.

00:24:00.910 --> 00:24:02.800
We have the flexibility
in terms of services

00:24:02.800 --> 00:24:04.060
on to provide the data.

00:24:04.060 --> 00:24:07.090
We've added our own API
on top of BigQuery API.

00:24:07.090 --> 00:24:08.770
So we could provide
programmatic access

00:24:08.770 --> 00:24:12.040
to our users that require that.

00:24:12.040 --> 00:24:15.520
And as the needs require,
we can scale up both storage

00:24:15.520 --> 00:24:18.370
and compute to meet-- to
satisfy the needs as they grow.

00:24:25.529 --> 00:24:27.570
And here the numbers kind
of speak for themselves

00:24:27.570 --> 00:24:29.580
in terms of the amount
of storage we have,

00:24:29.580 --> 00:24:32.190
the number of users, the amount
of users that uses [INAUDIBLE]

00:24:32.190 --> 00:24:35.430
in terms of queries
[INAUDIBLE] services.

00:24:35.430 --> 00:24:37.860
And really, the numbers kind
of speak to the significance

00:24:37.860 --> 00:24:40.650
of our system, kind of the
importance of it providing

00:24:40.650 --> 00:24:42.840
value for our company and
the day-to-day decisions

00:24:42.840 --> 00:24:45.030
that our colleagues
need to make.

00:24:45.030 --> 00:24:48.120
I think the numbers also speak
to the validity of our choice

00:24:48.120 --> 00:24:51.756
in using BigQuery as the
backbone of our infrastructure

00:24:51.756 --> 00:24:53.880
because it has been able
to grow to match the needs

00:24:53.880 --> 00:24:55.565
and demands of our services.

00:25:01.362 --> 00:25:02.820
So a couple of
learnings that we've

00:25:02.820 --> 00:25:05.530
had along the way, as
mentioned before, GDPR as well.

00:25:05.530 --> 00:25:08.580
I'm sure a lot of us had to
face that new regulation that

00:25:08.580 --> 00:25:13.690
took effect earlier this
year, which was so enjoyable.

00:25:13.690 --> 00:25:16.260
But for us it really presented
a very daunting proposition

00:25:16.260 --> 00:25:19.920
of having to restate petabytes
of data within our warehouse

00:25:19.920 --> 00:25:22.290
at a fairly frequent rate,
which would have been

00:25:22.290 --> 00:25:26.100
very time consuming, very
resource consuming, obviously

00:25:26.100 --> 00:25:26.910
very costly.

00:25:26.910 --> 00:25:30.180
And so we had to find
alternatives to handle that.

00:25:30.180 --> 00:25:32.280
And looking at those
we were able to settle

00:25:32.280 --> 00:25:35.046
using BigQuery DML.

00:25:35.046 --> 00:25:36.420
And what that
allowed us to do is

00:25:36.420 --> 00:25:40.230
to apply massive scale updates
and deletes in our data.

00:25:40.230 --> 00:25:42.180
It didn't require us to
do any additional work

00:25:42.180 --> 00:25:43.980
in terms of
reprocessing the data

00:25:43.980 --> 00:25:45.399
or having to restage our data.

00:25:45.399 --> 00:25:46.190
Everything was set.

00:25:48.810 --> 00:25:50.610
The functions were
very performant to meet

00:25:50.610 --> 00:25:52.510
our expectations.

00:25:52.510 --> 00:25:54.724
We did work cooperatively
with the dev team

00:25:54.724 --> 00:25:56.140
to get enhancements
along the way,

00:25:56.140 --> 00:25:59.030
like being able to apply DML
to streaming tables, which

00:25:59.030 --> 00:26:01.039
is one of our requirements.

00:26:01.039 --> 00:26:03.080
And we kind of continue
to work with the dev team

00:26:03.080 --> 00:26:05.130
to try to improve the
process by looking at things

00:26:05.130 --> 00:26:07.200
such as clustering, which
we expect are going to yield

00:26:07.200 --> 00:26:08.408
additional performance gains.

00:26:12.917 --> 00:26:14.500
In terms of streaming,
obviously being

00:26:14.500 --> 00:26:18.070
able to ingest event-level
data into your warehouse

00:26:18.070 --> 00:26:19.790
is a huge advantage.

00:26:19.790 --> 00:26:22.140
And for us, we kind of
applied a lambda architecture

00:26:22.140 --> 00:26:26.680
where we overlay the real-time
data with batch data.

00:26:26.680 --> 00:26:29.196
And so if you're querying for
the last 10 minutes or last 10

00:26:29.196 --> 00:26:31.570
weeks of data, you're just
going to the same table, using

00:26:31.570 --> 00:26:34.570
the same query, essentially.

00:26:34.570 --> 00:26:36.720
But it makes you think
in different aspects.

00:26:36.720 --> 00:26:39.460
So you have to think in
terms of quotas and inputs

00:26:39.460 --> 00:26:41.740
and the amount of events and
the size of those events.

00:26:45.264 --> 00:26:46.680
Of course, nothing
comes for free.

00:26:46.680 --> 00:26:49.109
So what we experienced with
tables with heavy streaming

00:26:49.109 --> 00:26:51.150
ingestion, there was going
to be some performance

00:26:51.150 --> 00:26:53.200
impacts in terms of queries.

00:26:53.200 --> 00:26:55.260
So we had to work
to either overcome

00:26:55.260 --> 00:26:57.810
or be able to work
with those situations.

00:26:57.810 --> 00:27:00.390
So in some cases, we used
on [INAUDIBLE] hardware

00:27:00.390 --> 00:27:01.410
to offset some of that.

00:27:01.410 --> 00:27:03.690
In other cases we're
looking at alternatives,

00:27:03.690 --> 00:27:05.317
like micro-batching
ingestion, which

00:27:05.317 --> 00:27:06.900
we're hoping will
increase performance

00:27:06.900 --> 00:27:07.770
of those situations.

00:27:12.300 --> 00:27:14.240
I think the really
biggest discovery for us

00:27:14.240 --> 00:27:17.450
is the independence of the
quotas for a lot of the things

00:27:17.450 --> 00:27:19.370
that you can configure.

00:27:19.370 --> 00:27:22.820
Streaming ingestion quotas
versus batch ingestion quotas

00:27:22.820 --> 00:27:25.730
don't impact each other.

00:27:25.730 --> 00:27:29.030
Querying as well as ingestion
quotas don't impact each other.

00:27:29.030 --> 00:27:31.190
And what it really
gives you is a lot

00:27:31.190 --> 00:27:33.590
of flexibility, a lot
of on-demand flexibility

00:27:33.590 --> 00:27:36.290
to configure your
situation, especially

00:27:36.290 --> 00:27:39.300
in highly dynamic environments.

00:27:39.300 --> 00:27:41.420
So you can handle any
event that comes to you

00:27:41.420 --> 00:27:44.330
and be able to handle
those accordingly.

00:27:50.090 --> 00:27:51.760
So in the media
business we encounter

00:27:51.760 --> 00:27:54.206
a lot of high-profile,
high-volume events.

00:27:54.206 --> 00:27:55.330
There's late-breaking news.

00:27:55.330 --> 00:27:57.490
There's live sporting events.

00:27:57.490 --> 00:27:59.380
So we went in an
arrangement with the NFL

00:27:59.380 --> 00:28:01.125
to stream their content.

00:28:01.125 --> 00:28:02.500
And it was decided
that we wanted

00:28:02.500 --> 00:28:04.690
to provide real-time metrics
around the performance

00:28:04.690 --> 00:28:06.520
of that streaming.

00:28:06.520 --> 00:28:10.120
And for us, that
meant experience peaks

00:28:10.120 --> 00:28:12.700
in terms of events
and size of datas

00:28:12.700 --> 00:28:14.950
that we don't typically
have to encounter.

00:28:14.950 --> 00:28:21.140
So in looking at alternatives
to satisfy those requirements

00:28:21.140 --> 00:28:23.110
using the hardware
route, we really

00:28:23.110 --> 00:28:26.660
didn't have the runway to
procure, set up the hardware,

00:28:26.660 --> 00:28:28.660
not to mention, what do
you do with our hardware

00:28:28.660 --> 00:28:30.580
after the event is completed?

00:28:30.580 --> 00:28:32.830
And so we looked
to BigQuery being

00:28:32.830 --> 00:28:36.640
able to dynamically
increase our quotas,

00:28:36.640 --> 00:28:38.830
meet the expectations
of the events,

00:28:38.830 --> 00:28:41.549
turn down the quotas
at the end of that.

00:28:41.549 --> 00:28:43.090
And the best thing
of all is you only

00:28:43.090 --> 00:28:47.320
pay for what you use
during that time.

00:28:47.320 --> 00:28:52.330
We were able to handle that
engagement with no issues

00:28:52.330 --> 00:28:53.700
in a very short amount of time.

00:28:53.700 --> 00:28:56.026
And so overall it was a
very successful engagement.

00:29:01.380 --> 00:29:03.230
So what are we looking
to do from here?

00:29:03.230 --> 00:29:05.670
Obviously, we want to continue
to unlock the value of data

00:29:05.670 --> 00:29:07.632
that we expose in our platform.

00:29:07.632 --> 00:29:09.090
We're going to
increase the volume,

00:29:09.090 --> 00:29:11.340
looking at additional data
sets that can provide value

00:29:11.340 --> 00:29:14.100
for other parts of the company.

00:29:14.100 --> 00:29:18.120
Looking at additional GCP
services like machine language,

00:29:18.120 --> 00:29:20.100
being able to do things
like user segmentation

00:29:20.100 --> 00:29:23.710
and clustering in that platform.

00:29:23.710 --> 00:29:25.920
And then we're working
with the BigQuery team

00:29:25.920 --> 00:29:29.130
at Google on pre-releases
and alpha and beta versions

00:29:29.130 --> 00:29:32.150
of additional features,
which I won't mention here.

00:29:32.150 --> 00:29:34.779
Not to take away the thunder
from Tino and company,

00:29:34.779 --> 00:29:36.570
but they will be
announcing those, I think,

00:29:36.570 --> 00:29:38.740
shortly tomorrow.

00:29:38.740 --> 00:29:41.694
And with that, I'm done.

00:29:41.694 --> 00:29:42.690
[APPLAUSE]

00:29:42.690 --> 00:29:43.981
TINO TERESHKO: Thank you, Tuan.

00:29:46.012 --> 00:29:46.970
I will take everything.

00:29:46.970 --> 00:29:47.553
TUAN PHAM: OK.

00:29:50.875 --> 00:29:52.500
TINO TERESHKO: Thank
you so much, Tuan.

00:29:52.500 --> 00:29:59.027
So the impact of the NFL game
event shouldn't be understated.

00:29:59.027 --> 00:30:01.110
Tuan and [? Cole ?] came
to us and said, hey, guys

00:30:01.110 --> 00:30:01.950
we're going to--

00:30:01.950 --> 00:30:03.600
we got this NFL game
going on and we're

00:30:03.600 --> 00:30:07.050
going to stream four
gigabytes per second

00:30:07.050 --> 00:30:10.000
into your streaming system.

00:30:10.000 --> 00:30:13.420
And, you know, we freaked
out a little bit at first,

00:30:13.420 --> 00:30:17.890
but they gave us enough lead
time and we delivered it.

00:30:17.890 --> 00:30:20.970
The important thing to note
is that all the complexity

00:30:20.970 --> 00:30:22.460
was on our side.

00:30:22.460 --> 00:30:25.650
All Tuan's team was looking
at is an API surface.

00:30:25.650 --> 00:30:27.845
So we went out there
and we made sure

00:30:27.845 --> 00:30:29.220
that everything
would work right.

00:30:29.220 --> 00:30:30.600
Our SREs were on call.

00:30:30.600 --> 00:30:33.440
Everyone was very
happy with the outcome.

00:30:33.440 --> 00:30:36.170
And they just sent
[INAUDIBLE] to us.

00:30:36.170 --> 00:30:38.524
And then they paid for
only what they consumed.

00:30:38.524 --> 00:30:39.440
So it's a great story.

00:30:39.440 --> 00:30:44.370
And the [INAUDIBLE],, which says,
as you get to these scales,

00:30:44.370 --> 00:30:47.459
the anti-pattern space
expands dramatically.

00:30:47.459 --> 00:30:50.000
There are so many things that
are unknowns that you have to--

00:30:50.000 --> 00:30:52.910
that you encounter for the
first time that you have to fix.

00:30:52.910 --> 00:30:56.940
So folks like Spotify
and folks like Oath,

00:30:56.940 --> 00:31:00.000
you know, the problems
that they face and they

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:03.610
work with us to resolve,
straighten our system.

00:31:03.610 --> 00:31:05.640
So people who aren't
necessarily at that scale

00:31:05.640 --> 00:31:09.610
get a more robust,
more performing system.

00:31:09.610 --> 00:31:14.400
Well, now I'm going to kind of
change the gears a little bit.

00:31:14.400 --> 00:31:17.180
Spotify and Oath have
been with us for a while.

00:31:17.180 --> 00:31:18.780
They're operating
on a huge scale,

00:31:18.780 --> 00:31:20.920
but we have a newcomer
into our platform.

00:31:20.920 --> 00:31:23.220
I believe in April
Twitter announced

00:31:23.220 --> 00:31:26.010
that they chose Google Cloud
Platform as their vendor

00:31:26.010 --> 00:31:30.570
of choice or cloud
vendor of choice

00:31:30.570 --> 00:31:33.000
to host their next-generation
data analytics platform.

00:31:33.000 --> 00:31:34.830
So it's early for them,
but they're already

00:31:34.830 --> 00:31:35.850
getting some results.

00:31:35.850 --> 00:31:38.430
So I'd like to welcome
a couple of engineers

00:31:38.430 --> 00:31:41.790
from Twitter who are going to
be talking about how they're

00:31:41.790 --> 00:31:43.450
using BigQuery today.

00:31:43.450 --> 00:31:46.670
Please welcome Pawan and Roman.

00:31:46.670 --> 00:31:49.490
[APPLAUSE]

00:31:51.717 --> 00:31:53.050
ROMAN DOVGOPOL: Thank you, Tino.

00:31:55.750 --> 00:31:57.260
PAWAN VALLURI: Hey
there, I'm Pawan.

00:31:57.260 --> 00:31:58.560
ROMAN DOVGOPOL: And I'm Roman.

00:31:58.560 --> 00:32:00.476
PAWAN VALLURI: We're
from the revenue platform

00:32:00.476 --> 00:32:01.277
team at Twitter.

00:32:01.277 --> 00:32:03.610
ROMAN DOVGOPOL: And today
we're going to talk about some

00:32:03.610 --> 00:32:07.390
of the compelling technical
challenges we faced recently.

00:32:07.390 --> 00:32:09.449
PAWAN VALLURI: How BigQuery
helped us solve them.

00:32:09.449 --> 00:32:10.990
ROMAN DOVGOPOL: And
where we go next.

00:32:14.825 --> 00:32:17.520
So at Twitter we
have a track record

00:32:17.520 --> 00:32:20.610
of BigQuery solving some of
the problems that have been

00:32:20.610 --> 00:32:23.300
impossible to solve before.

00:32:23.300 --> 00:32:33.180
And we really want
to tell a story

00:32:33.180 --> 00:32:34.890
of how we started
to use BigQuery

00:32:34.890 --> 00:32:36.780
and what drove us to get here.

00:32:40.860 --> 00:32:43.740
10 billion advertised
events process daily.

00:32:43.740 --> 00:32:45.570
So this is the scale
we have to deal with.

00:32:49.690 --> 00:32:53.840
And what happens to the scale
when something goes wrong?

00:32:53.840 --> 00:32:56.850
It's really hard to tell.

00:32:56.850 --> 00:32:59.940
So we had a need to
write arbitrary queries

00:32:59.940 --> 00:33:04.110
against our data sets to
answer some of these questions.

00:33:04.110 --> 00:33:08.280
And for a lot of the
data sources we own,

00:33:08.280 --> 00:33:12.650
it's either impossible or
extremely time consuming.

00:33:12.650 --> 00:33:15.610
So with that in
mind, I want to share

00:33:15.610 --> 00:33:19.540
one of the real-life cases.

00:33:19.540 --> 00:33:22.230
And I'll pass it to him.

00:33:22.230 --> 00:33:27.520
PAWAN VALLURI: So two
quarters ago we had a bug.

00:33:27.520 --> 00:33:32.620
Someone reported we showed
ads to the wrong user--

00:33:32.620 --> 00:33:35.060
something we should never do.

00:33:35.060 --> 00:33:38.500
So first steps, identify if
there are any other campaigns

00:33:38.500 --> 00:33:40.920
that were affected.

00:33:40.920 --> 00:33:43.570
Fortunately, we have
tools to do that.

00:33:43.570 --> 00:33:47.690
It's called business
audit records.

00:33:47.690 --> 00:33:50.120
Any change made to a
campaign gets logged

00:33:50.120 --> 00:33:51.680
into this table in MySQL.

00:33:51.680 --> 00:33:55.320
And so all you have
to do is to query it.

00:33:55.320 --> 00:33:59.280
Turns out it's really
hard to do at our scale.

00:33:59.280 --> 00:34:01.790
We own one of Twitter's
largest MySQL testers,

00:34:01.790 --> 00:34:04.350
and this is one of
its largest tables.

00:34:04.350 --> 00:34:07.170
You can literally hear
MySQL scream in pain

00:34:07.170 --> 00:34:10.284
when you're trying to query
a 3.2 billion row table.

00:34:13.030 --> 00:34:18.639
So how long did it take for us
to finally answer the question?

00:34:18.639 --> 00:34:22.150
Were there any other
campaigns that were affected?

00:34:22.150 --> 00:34:24.159
It took us two weeks.

00:34:24.159 --> 00:34:27.709
It took us two weeks,
four engineers, a DBA,

00:34:27.709 --> 00:34:32.520
and a whole lot of pain to
finally answer the question.

00:34:32.520 --> 00:34:36.090
How could we do better?

00:34:36.090 --> 00:34:39.210
The platform team--
the data platform team

00:34:39.210 --> 00:34:41.400
has been experimenting
with BigQuery for a while,

00:34:41.400 --> 00:34:43.440
and we thought it was
the perfect technology

00:34:43.440 --> 00:34:46.199
to help solve our problem.

00:34:46.199 --> 00:34:48.370
With the power of BQ
Blaster that Roman

00:34:48.370 --> 00:34:53.370
is going to talk about next, our
data was ingested in no time.

00:34:53.370 --> 00:34:56.969
And now things are as
simple as writing SQL,

00:34:56.969 --> 00:34:58.800
but without the timeouts.

00:34:58.800 --> 00:35:03.980
This takes less than
a minute to execute.

00:35:03.980 --> 00:35:08.514
And this was just one use case.

00:35:08.514 --> 00:35:10.930
ROMAN DOVGOPOL: So we looked
at this use case and a couple

00:35:10.930 --> 00:35:12.310
of other test cases.

00:35:12.310 --> 00:35:16.270
And we really saw the
potential to answer questions

00:35:16.270 --> 00:35:20.335
in more depth if we move all
our data sources to BigQuery.

00:35:22.860 --> 00:35:25.770
And here's where it becomes
a really hard engineering

00:35:25.770 --> 00:35:26.550
problem.

00:35:26.550 --> 00:35:29.820
How do we generalize
the ingestion regardless

00:35:29.820 --> 00:35:32.172
of the source and its scale.

00:35:34.880 --> 00:35:37.430
It was never an easy
transition for us.

00:35:37.430 --> 00:35:40.670
BigQuery alone was not enough.

00:35:40.670 --> 00:35:43.400
And we really had to
build a set of tools

00:35:43.400 --> 00:35:46.907
to enable those experiments,
to see what queries we can run,

00:35:46.907 --> 00:35:48.990
and also with new questions
we could get answered.

00:35:52.670 --> 00:35:55.160
BigQuery blaster or BQ
blaster, how we call for short,

00:35:55.160 --> 00:35:57.745
is one of the first tool
that were built for this.

00:35:57.745 --> 00:35:58.870
And let's see what it does.

00:36:02.780 --> 00:36:05.530
We started with introspection
on [INAUDIBLE] systems.

00:36:05.530 --> 00:36:10.840
And with so many data
sources and database types,

00:36:10.840 --> 00:36:14.350
we really wanted to build a
tool that would abstract away

00:36:14.350 --> 00:36:15.440
the original data source.

00:36:20.480 --> 00:36:25.130
So the initiator for this would
be a scouting job that picks up

00:36:25.130 --> 00:36:29.235
the data from any original
source that we have

00:36:29.235 --> 00:36:31.060
and lands in a
Google Cloud Storage.

00:36:34.640 --> 00:36:38.387
The very last step is
a BigQuery import job

00:36:38.387 --> 00:36:40.220
that picks up data from
Google Cloud Storage

00:36:40.220 --> 00:36:42.110
and lands it straight
to BigQuery table.

00:36:46.750 --> 00:36:49.360
So this is the
tool in a nutshell.

00:36:49.360 --> 00:36:52.550
We also want to talk
about where we go next.

00:36:52.550 --> 00:36:55.050
PAWAN VALLURI: So we took the
learnings from this initiative

00:36:55.050 --> 00:36:58.820
and are applying it
to our next challenge.

00:36:58.820 --> 00:37:02.150
ROMAN DOVGOPOL: Getting the
data [? organization ?] right.

00:37:02.150 --> 00:37:06.790
PAWAN VALLURI: We consume
more than 100 terabyte data

00:37:06.790 --> 00:37:09.970
of revenue logs per day.

00:37:09.970 --> 00:37:12.940
ROMAN DOVGOPOL: Moving
all of this to BigQuery

00:37:12.940 --> 00:37:17.035
is our next challenge, and we
know how to tackle this now.

00:37:17.035 --> 00:37:19.510
PAWAN VALLURI: Thank you.

00:37:19.510 --> 00:37:22.480
[APPLAUSE]

